tainer class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"148\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D458 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2260\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c38\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi><mo>\u2260</mo><mn>8.</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement II is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.  </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Finally, only statement I is true.     </p>\n</div></div>",
